1. Frontend, Backend, and Full-Stack Development

Web development can be divided into three major parts — frontend, backend, and full-stack development.
The frontend refers to the part of a website that users directly see and interact with. It includes everything visible on the browser such as layout, buttons, text, colors, and navigation menus. Technologies commonly used in frontend development are HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, along with modern frameworks like React or Angular. For example, when you visit an online store, the product listings, images, and “Add to Cart” buttons are all part of the frontend.

The backend is the hidden part of a website that handles logic, database management, and communication between the server and the client. It ensures that data entered by users is processed correctly and stored securely. Backend development involves technologies like Node.js, Python, Java, PHP, and databases such as MySQL or MongoDB. For instance, when you place an order on an e-commerce site, the backend processes your order details and stores them in a database.

A full-stack developer is someone who can handle both frontend and backend development. They are capable of designing user interfaces, building server-side logic, and managing databases — essentially developing the entire web application from start to finish. For example, a full-stack developer can create both the product page you see and the backend system that updates inventory or processes payments.

2. The client (browser) sends an HTTP request to the server.
The server processes it and sends back the requested data or web page.

3. How a Browser Requests and Displays a Web Page

The user enters a URL (e.g., https://example.com).
The browser sends an HTTP request to the web server.
The server locates the requested page (like index.html).
The server sends back an HTTP response containing HTML, CSS, and JavaScript
The browser’s rendering engine parses and displays the page visually.
Additional assets (images, videos, scripts) are loaded as needed.

